## Growth on the health affiliate site before the drop {% #growth %}

The health affiliate site is a site in my own portfolio. I'm Sunny Patel, I own and run it, and this page is not client work. Google Search Console data, pulled on 4 October 2026, begins on 22 March 2026. Between then and 19 July 2026 the health affiliate site grew from a few clicks a day to a peak of 41 clicks on 25 June. Google clicks were 24 in the last ten days of March, 174 in April, 104 in May and 476 in June, and from 1 to 19 July the site averaged about 19 clicks a day.

{% chart type="bar" eyebrow="Google impressions" title="Google impressions for the health affiliate site before the drop: 22 to 31 March, April, May, June and 1 to 19 July 2026" data="22 to 31 Mar:3645|Apr:26547|May:32153|Jun:44193|1 to 19 Jul:27956" highlight="Jun" source="Google Search Console via SEO Gets, 22 March to 19 July 2026, pulled 4 Oct 2026" /%}

Google impressions for the health affiliate site rose from 3,645 in the last ten days of March to 44,193 in June 2026. The first and last bars cover part-months, 10 and 19 days, so they are not comparable with the full months between them. May was lower than April on clicks (104 against 174) even though impressions were higher, so the growth was not a straight line.

## The 20 July drop in Google impressions {% #drop %}

Google impressions for the health affiliate site fell by 96% from 20 July 2026. They averaged about 1,630 a day from 15 to 19 July, including 1,583 on 19 July. On 20 July they were 225 and on 21 July 148, and from 22 to 31 July they averaged 66 a day. Daily Google clicks went from about 21 to about 1 over the same comparison. The on-site changes I describe below were made on 2 and 3 August 2026, about two weeks after the fall, so they did not cause it.

{% chart type="line" eyebrow="Google impressions" title="Daily Google impressions for the health affiliate site, 15 July to 3 August 2026" data="15 Jul:1627|16 Jul:1694|17 Jul:1624|18 Jul:1631|19 Jul:1583|20 Jul:225|21 Jul:148|22 Jul:77|23 Jul:48|24 Jul:81|25 Jul:73|26 Jul:97|27 Jul:73|28 Jul:61|29 Jul:56|30 Jul:48|31 Jul:45|1 Aug:70|2 Aug:54|3 Aug:58" source="Google Search Console via SEO Gets, 15 July to 3 August 2026, pulled 4 Oct 2026" /%}

Search Console recorded two earlier one-to-two-day dips on the health affiliate site, on 4 to 5 July and 12 to 13 July 2026, and both recovered within a day or two. The 20 July fall has not recovered: August 2026 totalled 1,870 impressions and 15 clicks, and September 2026 totalled 1,032 impressions and 11 clicks, against 44,193 impressions and 476 clicks in June.

The fall is specific to Google. Comparing 6 to 19 July with 20 July to 2 August 2026, GA4 organic sessions on the health affiliate site from Google fell from 344 to 41. Over the same two periods Bing rose from 308 to 444, DuckDuckGo from 464 to 652 and Yahoo from 149 to 234. I covered the same site alongside other sites in my [21-site Search Console review](/blog/google-update-portfolio-impact/).

## AI referrals to the health affiliate site over the same period {% #ai %}

AI assistant visits to the health affiliate site did not fall when Google did, but they fell about two weeks later. GA4 attributed between 101 and 144 sessions a week to AI assistants from the week starting 1 June to the week starting 27 July 2026, an average of 127. In the week starting 3 August the figure dropped to 78, then 42, 34 and 21, and from the week starting 10 August to the week starting 21 September it averaged 28 a week, 78% lower than before. A session here is a recorded visit.

{% chart type="line" eyebrow="AI assistant sessions" title="Weekly sessions attributed to AI assistants on the health affiliate site, weeks starting 1 June to 21 September 2026" data="1 Jun:112|8 Jun:142|15 Jun:128|22 Jun:131|29 Jun:144|6 Jul:134|13 Jul:101|20 Jul:118|27 Jul:136|3 Aug:78|10 Aug:42|17 Aug:34|24 Aug:21|31 Aug:28|7 Sep:23|14 Sep:23|21 Sep:22" source="GA4, 1 June to 27 September 2026, pulled 4 Oct 2026" /%}

Monthly AI assistant sessions on the health affiliate site were 553 in June, 556 in July, 214 in August and 98 in September 2026, so the assistants did not hold up either. The decline began in the days around my on-site changes of 2 and 3 August, and I do not know whether the two are connected. Over the 91 days from 3 July to 1 October 2026, the health affiliate site received 824 AI assistant sessions: ChatGPT 641 (including 4 labelled OpenAI), Perplexity 88, Copilot 58 and Claude 37. Google organic search sent 480 sessions in the same window.

{% chart type="bar" eyebrow="AI assistant sessions" title="Sessions attributed to AI assistants on the health affiliate site, 3 July to 1 October 2026, 824 in total" data="ChatGPT:641|Perplexity:88|Copilot:58|Claude:37" suffix=" sessions" highlight="ChatGPT" source="GA4, 3 July to 1 October 2026, pulled 4 Oct 2026" /%}

These are visits attributed by GA4 to assistant sources. They are not citation counts or measures of answer visibility, and I have not tested whether assistants stopped linking to the health affiliate site or which pages they linked to.

## What I have and have not fixed on the health affiliate site {% #fixed %}

On 2 and 3 August 2026 I made two changes to the health affiliate site. I removed an invented expert author byline and the first-hand testing claims attached to it, which I could not support, and I set the structured data author to the organisation. I also redirected 12 near-duplicate condition pages into the main set of condition pages.

Neither change has brought Google impressions back. September 2026 impressions were 1,032 against 44,193 in June. Search Console shows no manual action against the health affiliate site. A sudden fall that affects only Google fits a site-level quality assessment, but Google has not told me why, and I cannot show which change, if either, matters. The cause is not confirmed. I have paused adding content to the health affiliate site until I know more, and I make no recovery claim and no revenue claim.

## Questions about these results {% #faq %}

### Has the health affiliate site recovered in Google?

No. Google Search Console shows 1,032 impressions and 11 clicks for the health affiliate site in September 2026, against 44,193 impressions and 476 clicks in June 2026. About eleven weeks after the fall began on 20 July, there is no sign of recovery in the data pulled on 4 October 2026.

### Did removing the author byline and redirecting pages cause the fall in AI assistant visits?

That is not established. AI assistant sessions on the health affiliate site started to fall in the week beginning 3 August 2026, the same days as the changes on 2 and 3 August, but there was no control site and no way to separate the two. The fall may also have followed the Google drop with a delay for a reason I have not identified.

### Does a Google demotion also reduce AI assistant visits?

On this one site it did, with a lag of about two weeks. One site cannot establish a mechanism, and I have not tested why assistants sent fewer visits. I would not generalise from it, and I would measure Google, other search engines and AI assistant visits separately on any site.

### Was this a Google penalty?

Search Console shows no manual action against the health affiliate site. The drop was sudden and affected only Google, while Bing, DuckDuckGo and Yahoo sessions rose. I describe it as a demotion because the data shows lost visibility, not because I can name a cause.

